  <section xml:id="sec-mics-multilevel">
    <title>Multilevel Data in R: Kenya MICS</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Now let's get our hands on some real multilevel data in R.
        The Kenya Multiple Indicator Cluster Survey (MICS) is a nationally
        representative household survey, run by UNICEF and the Kenya National
        Bureau of Statistics.
        Like most modern data, it's built around a multilevel structure:
        children are nested within women, women within household members, and
        household members within households.
      </p>

      <p>
        The data is free, but you do need to register to get it.
        To follow along with the R code in this section:
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              Go to
              <url href="https://mics.unicef.org/surveys">https://mics.unicef.org/surveys</url>
              and select Kenya.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Register for a free account and request access to the microdata.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Download the dataset, and put the <c>.sav</c> files in your
              working directory.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>

      <p>
        If you're still waiting for access, don't worry.
        Read through this section anyway, because the ideas apply to any
        multilevel dataset.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-mics-files">
      <title>Four files, four levels</title>

      <p>
        The Kenya MICS comes as four separate data files, one for each level of
        the hierarchy:
      </p>

      <tabular halign="left">
        <col halign="left" width="15%"/>
        <col halign="left" width="15%"/>
        <col halign="left" width="35%"/>
        <col halign="right" width="10%"/>
        <col halign="left" width="25%"/>
        <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
          <cell>File</cell>
          <cell>Level</cell>
          <cell>Description</cell>
          <cell>Rows</cell>
          <cell>What each row represents</cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell><c>hhKE.sav</c></cell>
          <cell>Household</cell>
          <cell>
            Information on household, so therefore at household level - e.g.,
            material of roof, if there's a latrine, etc
          </cell>
          <cell>8,993</cell>
          <cell>One household</cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell><c>hlKE.sav</c></cell>
          <cell>Household member</cell>
          <cell>
            Information on each household member, therefore, you can have
            multiple household level variables here, but they would be repeated
            for all members in a household.
            Instead that household level information is given in the data set
            about.
            This is just information on household members - e.g., age, sex,
            relationship to household head
          </cell>
          <cell>46,038</cell>
          <cell>One person living in a surveyed household</cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell><c>wmKE.sav</c></cell>
          <cell>Woman</cell>
          <cell>
            This is also at the household member level, and contains additional
            information for women aged 15-49 within a household.
            This is given in it's own data set as a lot of the questions would
            be irrelevant for the whole household - e.g., fertility status
          </cell>
          <cell>10,525</cell>
          <cell>One woman aged 15 <ndash/> 49 in a surveyed household</cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell><c>chKE.sav</c></cell>
          <cell>Child</cell>
          <cell>
            This is also at the household member level, and contains additional
            information for children under 5 within a household.
            This is given in it's own data set as a lot of the questions would
            be irrelevant for the whole household - e.g., height, weight, and
            vaccination status.
          </cell>
          <cell>7,266</cell>
          <cell>One child under 5 in a surveyed household</cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>

      <p>
        You'll notice the row counts aren't neat multiples of each other.
        That makes sense when you think about it: not every household has a
        child under five, not every woman has children, and not every household
        member is a woman aged 15 to 49.
      </p>

      <p>
        We can load all four files into R with the <c>haven</c> package, which
        reads SPSS <c>.sav</c> files:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        library(haven)
        library(dplyr)

        hh &lt;- read_sav("hhKE.sav")   # households
        hl &lt;- read_sav("hlKE.sav")   # household members
        wm &lt;- read_sav("wmKE.sav")   # women
        ch &lt;- read_sav("chKE.sav")   # children under 5

        # How many rows in each file?
        nrow(hh)
        #&gt; [1] 8993
        nrow(hl)
        #&gt; [1] 46038
        nrow(wm)
        #&gt; [1] 10525
        nrow(ch)
        #&gt; [1] 7266
        </code>
      </program>

      <insight xml:id="ss-mics-variables">
        <title>Understanding the variable names</title>

        <p>
          Open any of the four files, and the first thing you'll notice is that
          the column names don't tell you very much.
          The household file has variables called <c>HI1</c>, <c>HI2</c>,
          <c>HI3D</c>, <c>HI3M</c>, <c>HI3Y</c>, <c>HI10</c>, <c>HIMEM</c>, and
          so on.
          This is really common in large household surveys.
          The names are short codes that are unambiguous and consistent across
          countries and survey rounds, but they tell you very little about
          what's in the variable.
        </p>

        <p>
          There's a good, practical reason for this.
          MICS surveys run in dozens of countries, often in several languages.
          A short code like <c>HI1</c> is language-neutral, so it's the same in
          the French and English versions of a survey.
          Long descriptive names would change from language to language, and
          make it much harder to automate comparisons across countries.
          The price you pay is that the names mean little without the
          documentation.
        </p>

        <p>
          Luckily, the Word document that comes with the Kenya MICS download
          gives you the translation.
          <c>HI1</c> is the cluster number.
          <c>HI2</c> is the household number within the cluster.
          <c>HI3D</c>, <c>HI3M</c>, and <c>HI3Y</c> are the day, month, and year
          of interview.
          <c>HI10</c> is the result of the household interview.
          <c>HIMEM</c> is the number of household members.
          Keep that document close by, because you'll need it whenever an
          unfamiliar variable name comes up (and there are a lot of them!).
        </p>

        <p>
          Even better, the <c>haven</c> package keeps the variable labels from
          the <c>.sav</c> file when you read it into R, so you don't always need
          to look them up yourself.
          The labels are attached to each column, and you can see them directly:
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
      # See the label for one variable
      attr(hh$HI1, "label")
      #&gt; [1] "Cluster number"

      attr(hh$HIMEM, "label")
      #&gt; [1] "Number of household members"

      # See labels for all variables at once
      labelled::var_label(hh)
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          The screenshot below shows what this looks like if you open the data
          in the RStudio viewer ( <c>View(hh)</c> ).
          The short variable codes are the column headers, and the descriptive
          labels sit in a second row underneath.
          In R, the labels are stored as metadata attached to each column
          instead of as a visible row, and you can get to them whenever you need
          them.
        </p>

        <figure xml:id="fig-mics-variable-labels">
          <caption>
            Variable names and labels from the Kenya MICS household file, as
            displayed in a spreadsheet.
            Short codes like <c>HI1</c> and <c>HIMEM</c> are the actual column
            names; the descriptive labels below them are stored as metadata and
            preserved by the <c>haven</c> package when the file is read into R.
          </caption>

          <image source="images/ch2_mics_variable_labels.png" width="100%">
            <description>
              <p>
                A spreadsheet showing the top two rows of the Kenya MICS
                household file.
                The first row contains short variable codes: HI1, HI2, HI3D,
                HI3M, HI3Y, HI10, HIMEM.
                The second row contains descriptive labels in orange text:
                Cluster number, Household number, Day of interview, Month of
                interview, Year of interview, Result of household interview,
                Number of household members.
              </p>
            </description>
          </image>
        </figure>
      </insight>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-mics-level-mapping">
      <title>Mapping the levels</title>

      <p>
        Before we analyse any multilevel dataset, we find it really helpful to
        do a quick level-mapping exercise.
        Work out what units there are at each level, how they connect, and which
        variables are measured at each level.
        For the Kenya MICS, the map looks like this:
      </p>

      <tabular halign="left">
        <col halign="left" width="20%"/>
        <col halign="left" width="25%"/>
        <col halign="left" width="55%"/>
        <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
          <cell>Level</cell>
          <cell>Unit</cell>
          <cell>Example variables measured here</cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell>4 (highest)</cell>
          <cell>Household</cell>
          <cell>
            Water source, Floor materials, Province, Wealth index, ...
          </cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell>3</cell>
          <cell>Household Members</cell>
          <cell>Sex, Age, Education level, ...</cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell>2</cell>
          <cell>Woman</cell>
          <cell>
            Age, Education, Number of children born, care during pregnancy, ...
          </cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell>1 (lowest)</cell>
          <cell>Child under 5</cell>
          <cell>Age in months, Health, Vaccination history, ...</cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>

      <p>
        Even this simple map raises questions that shape the analysis.
        Say we're interested in a child's nutritional status, for example
        whether they're short for their age.
        That outcome lives at Level 1.
        But the things that might explain it live at every level: the child's
        own age and sex (Level 1), their mother's education (Level 2), the
        education of other people in the household (Level 3), and the
        household's wealth, water source, and whether it's in an urban or rural
        area (Level 4).
        Leave out any one of these levels, and you risk missing something
        important.
      </p>

      <p>
        Here are the key questions we ask when mapping any multilevel dataset:
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              How many units are there at each level?
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              What variable links each level to the one above it?
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Are group sizes roughly equal, or do some groups have many more
              members than others?
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              What variables are measured at each level?
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              At which level does the outcome of interest live, and at which
              levels do the potential explanatory variables live?
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
        We can take the household members data and find information on the
        number of members per household (on average).
        Note that <c>HID</c> is the household identifier, so individuals within
        the same household would have the same <c>HID</c>.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        # How many household members per household?
        hl %&gt;%
          group_by(HID) %&gt;%
          summarise(n_members = n()) %&gt;%
          summarise(
            mean_size = mean(n_members),
            min_size  = min(n_members),
            max_size  = max(n_members)
          )
        #&gt; # A tibble: 1 x 3
        #&gt;   mean_size min_size max_size
        #&gt;       &lt;dbl&gt;    &lt;int&gt;    &lt;int&gt;
        #&gt; 1      5.12        1       23
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        One average, there are 5.12 surveyed individuals within a household.
        Some households have just one, and at least one has 23 members surveyed!
        Similarly, we can look at the number of children (5 years or younger)
        per household:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        # How many children per household, among households
        # with at least one child?
        ch %&gt;%
          group_by(HID) %&gt;%
          summarise(n_children = n()) %&gt;%
          count(n_children)
        #&gt; # A tibble: 6 x 2
        #&gt; n_children     n
        #&gt;      &lt;int&gt; &lt;int&gt;
        #&gt; 1  3040
        #&gt; 2  1579
        #&gt; 3   286
        #&gt; 4    42
        #&gt; 5     6
        #&gt; 6     2
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        Most households have just a few children, but there are some with many:
        3040 households have one child, but two households have six children!
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-mics-linking">
      <title>How the levels connect, and where they don't</title>

      <introduction>
        <p>
          The files are linked by a shared household identifier, <c>HID</c>.
          This allows us to connect information across the different levels of
          the survey data.
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
      # How many unique households appear in each file?
      n_distinct(hh$HID)
      #&gt; [1] 8993
      n_distinct(hl$HID)
      #&gt; [1] 8989
      n_distinct(wm$HID)
      #&gt; [1] 7787
      n_distinct(ch$HID)
      #&gt; [1] 4955
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          At the household level, there are 8993 unique households.
          This checks out, as we previously saw there are 8993 rows, and we know
          this should be one household per row.
          At the household members level, there are slightly fewer unique
          households (8989), indicating that four households have no members
          listed (we dig into this in <xref ref="ch-cleaning-recoding"/> ).
          At the women's level, there are even fewer unique households (7787),
          indicating that many households did not have any eligible women (women
          aged 15-49).
          At the children's level, there are only 4955 unique households,
          indicating that many households did not have any eligible children
          (children under 5).
        </p>
      </introduction>

      <subsubsection xml:id="ss-mics-trace">
        <title>Tracing one household through all four files</title>

        <p>
          A good exercise to help understand a new multilevel dataset for the
          first time is to follow one unit all the way through every level.
          Pick a household, and find every record that relates to it across all
          four files.
          It really helps make the structure concrete.
        </p>

        <p>
          To see all four files in action, we need a household that has at least
          one woman aged 15 to 49 and at least one child under 5.
          The variables <c>HI12</c> and <c>HI13</c> in the household file give
          us these counts, so we can take the first household that has both:
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
      example_hid &lt;- (hh %&gt;%
        filter(HI12 &gt; 0 &amp; HI13 &gt; 0) %&gt;%
        pull(HID))[1]

      example_hid
      #&gt; [1] 2078
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          Now let's pull out every record for household 2078 from each file:
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
      # Household-level record
      hh %&gt;% filter(HID == example_hid)

      # Everyone in the household
      hl %&gt;% filter(HID == example_hid)
      #&gt; # A tibble: 7 x ...
      # Seven people live in this household.

      # Women aged 15-49 in the household
      wm %&gt;% filter(HID == example_hid)
      #&gt; # A tibble: 2 x ...
      # Two of those seven are women aged 15-49.

      # Children under 5 in the household
      ch %&gt;% filter(HID == example_hid)
      #&gt; # A tibble: 1 x ...
      # One child under 5.
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          Household 2078 has seven members.
          Two of them are women aged 15 to 49, so they each have a record in the
          women's file.
          One is a child under 5, who has a record in the children's file.
          The other four people (maybe older children, men, or elderly
          relatives) only appear in the household members file, where we can
          check their ages and sex.
          Putting the four files together, here's some of what we know about
          household 2078:
          <ul>
            <li>
              The household record shows that they live in Central Kenya, and
              are in the 4th wealth quintile (where 5 is the richest).
              They have water piped into their house, and a traditional pit
              latrine.
            </li>

            <li>
              The women's records gives that the two 15-49 year old women that
              live here have primary-level education.
              The 25-year-old has given birth, and the 18-year-old hasn't.
            </li>

            <li>
              The under-5 record gives details of the 25-year-olds child.
              They have a daughter who weighs 7.2 kg and is 66 cm long.
              She has had some vaccinations.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </p>

        <p>
          Think about what that means for the variables.
          Household-level variables, like water source, wealth index, or
          sanitation, apply equally to all people in the household.
          This data is therefore at the household level.
          Then information on individual household members is given in the
          household-members file.
          Women's (15-49) and children's (less than 5 years old) information is
          still at the household members level.
          But, these data sets contain extra information related to just women
          aged 15-49, or children less than or equal to 5 years old,
          respectively, so warrant their own data set.
        </p>
      </subsubsection>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-mics-wrong-level">
      <title>What goes wrong when you ignore the levels</title>

      <p>
        Let's say you want to find out whether urban children are better
        nourished than rural children.
        Whether a child lives in an urban or rural area depends on their
        household's cluster, so that's a cluster-level variable.
        How well nourished they are is a child-level variable.
        These two things live at different levels.
      </p>

      <p>
        Now suppose you ignore that.
        You merge the cluster variable onto every child's row, and run an
        analysis that treats every child as an independent observation.
        You'll get an answer, but it'll be overconfident.
        Children in the same cluster share a location, a local food system, a
        water supply, and local health services, so they aren't independent of
        each other.
        If you treat them as if they are, your analysis thinks it has more
        independent information than it really has, and confidence intervals
        that should be wide end up looking narrow.
      </p>

      <insight>
        <p>
          For example, a household with eight members contributes more rows to
          the dataset than a household with two, and if you ignore the nesting,
          that can distort even simple summaries.
          Here's an example.
          Let's calculate the mean number of rooms per household, first using
          the <c>hh</c> data, and then using the <c>hl</c> data:
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
        mean(hh$SE4, na.rm = TRUE)
        #&gt; [1] 2.392829
        mean(hl$SE4, na.rm = TRUE)
        #&gt; [1] 2.507339
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          The answers are different!
          The mean number of rooms is higher when we calculate it from the
          individual-level data ( <c>hl</c> ) than from the household-level data
          ( <c>hh</c> ).
          That's because bigger households appear lots of times in <c>hl</c>,
          once for every household member, so their information gets repeated
          across many rows.
          If we don't account for the multilevel structure, those bigger
          households pull the mean up.
        </p>
      </insight>

      <p>
        This is the heart of the problem with multilevel structure, and it's
        exactly what multilevel methods are designed to deal with.
        Berkeley showed us one consequence of ignoring it: a conclusion that
        completely reversed once we took the groups into account.
        The MICS data shows us another: even when the conclusion doesn't flip,
        ignoring the groups gives you false precision.
        Either way, it's a really good reason to map out your levels before you
        start any analysis.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <!--  EXERCISES                                                        -->
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <exercises xml:id="exercises-multilevel-data">
    <title>Exercises</title>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-aggregation-consequences">
      <title>What gets lost when you aggregate?</title>

      <statement>
        <p>
          A researcher has data on student exam scores from 20 schools across 3
          counties in Kenya.
          They work out the average score across all the students in the
          dataset, and reports it as the county average.
        </p>

        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              What have they assumed, without saying so, by calculating a single
              average across all the students?
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Give one example of a situation where this assumption would lead
              them to the wrong conclusion.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              What would they need to do differently to get a valid county-level
              average?
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <p>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <p>
                They've assumed that every student contributes equally to the
                county picture, regardless of which school they're in.
                In other words, they've assumed school membership doesn't
                matter, and that schools with more students in the dataset
                shouldn't count for any more than schools with fewer.
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                Say one very large, high-performing school in a county has far
                more students than the other schools combined.
                Pooling all students together, that one school's scores would
                dominate the county average, even if most of the county's
                schools were actually performing poorly.
                The reported "county average" would really just be describing
                that one school.
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                Work out the average score within each school first, then
                average those school-level averages (or otherwise account for
                school size explicitly), rather than pooling every student's
                score into one calculation.
                That way, every school gets a fair say in the county figure,
                regardless of how many students it has.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-simpsons-paradox-spot">
      <title>Spotting the hidden variable</title>

      <statement>
        <p>
          A hospital reports that its overall patient survival rate went up from
          72% in 2020 to 78% in 2022.
          A journalist writes a story praising the hospital for improving its
          care.
        </p>

        <p>
          Then a data analyst breaks the survival rate down by ward, and finds
          this:
        </p>

        <tabular>
          <row header="yes">
            <cell>Ward</cell>
            <cell>2020 survival rate</cell>
            <cell>2022 survival rate</cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>General</cell>
            <cell>90%</cell>
            <cell>88%</cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>Intensive Care</cell>
            <cell>50%</cell>
            <cell>48%</cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>

        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              Survival rates went down in both wards between 2020 and 2022.
              So how could the overall survival rate go up?
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              What's the hidden variable that the journalist's story ignored?
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              What does this example have in common with the Berkeley admissions
              case from earlier in this chapter?
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <p>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <p>
                Survival can rise overall even though it fell in both wards, if
                the <em>mix</em> of patients across the two wards shifted
                between 2020 and 2022.
                If a larger share of patients were treated in General (where
                survival is much higher) in 2022 than in 2020, and a smaller
                share in Intensive Care, the overall figure would drift up
                towards General's rate, even with both wards individually
                getting slightly worse.
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                The hidden variable is which ward each patient was treated in,
                and specifically how the proportion of patients in each ward
                changed over time.
                A pooled survival rate hides that shift completely.
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                It's the same structure as Berkeley.
                A single headline number blends together groups (departments, or
                wards) that have very different rates and very different sizes.
                Judging fairness, or improvement, from the pooled number alone
                misses what's actually happening within each group, and can even
                point you in the wrong direction.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-multilevel-kuccps">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <xref ref="ss-kenya-relevance"/> mentions that KUCCPS applies
          Affirmative Action Criteria covering gender when placing students into
          degree programmes.
          Suppose a single national placement rate showed a gap between men and
          women.
          If you think about it the way the Berkeley statisticians did, what
          grouping variable would you want that national rate broken down by,
          before deciding whether the gap was down to unfair treatment or to
          differences in <em>where</em> people applied?
          What pattern in that breakdown would tell you the gap was a result of
          Simpson's paradox, rather than a real difference?
        </p>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <p>
          You'd want the national rate broken down by the programme (or
          institution) each person applied to, the same role that department
          played at Berkeley.
          Programmes differ hugely in how competitive they are and how many
          places they offer, so where people applied can matter as much as how
          they were treated once they got there.
        </p>

        <p>
          You'd suspect Simpson's paradox if, within most individual programmes,
          men and women were placed at roughly similar rates (or women even did
          better), while women were more concentrated in the more competitive,
          harder-to-enter programmes overall.
          That pattern would mean the national gap comes from <em>where</em>
          women tended to apply, not from unequal treatment within any given
          programme.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-multilevel-kidney-recap">
      <statement>
        <p>
          In the 1980s, doctors compared two treatments for kidney stones: open
          surgery, and a newer, less invasive procedure called percutaneous
          nephrolithotomy (PCNL) (Charig et al., 1986).
        </p>

        <p>
          Looking at all patients together, PCNL looked like the better option:
          it succeeded 82.6% of the time, compared with 78.0% for open surgery.
        </p>

        <p>
          But stone size matters for how difficult a case is to treat, so the
          researchers also looked separately at patients with small and large
          stones.
        </p>

        <p>
          For small stones, open surgery succeeded 93.1% of the time, compared
          with 86.7% for PCNL.
          For large stones, open surgery succeeded 73.0% of the time, compared
          with 68.8% for PCNL.
        </p>

        <p>
          So open surgery was more successful in both groups defined by stone
          size, but less successful overall.
        </p>

        <p>
          Let's work out why.
        </p>

        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              <term>What does the overall comparison suggest?</term>
            </p>

            <p>
              If you only looked at the overall success rates, which treatment
              would appear to be more successful?
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              <term>What happens when we take stone size into account?</term>
            </p>

            <p>
              Compare the treatments separately for small stones and large
              stones.
              Which treatment has the higher success rate in each group?
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              <term>So why does open surgery look worse overall?</term>
            </p>

            <p>
              The treatments were not used equally for all types of patients.
              Doctors tended to reserve open surgery for the harder cases.
              76.7% of patients with large stones received open surgery.
              Meanwhile, 75.6% of patients with small stones received PCNL.
            </p>

            <p>
              What does this tell us about the kinds of patients whose success
              rates were being combined for each treatment?
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              <term>Put the pieces together.</term>
            </p>

            <p>
              Open surgery was more successful within both stone-size groups.
              Why didn't that advantage appear when all patients were combined?
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              <term>What is the general lesson?</term>
            </p>

            <p>
              Stone size sits above the individual patient, in much the same way
              that department sat above the individual applicant in the Berkeley
              example.
            </p>

            <p>
              In your own words, explain why it can be misleading to compare
              treatments using one overall number when the groups receiving the
              treatments are different.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>

        <p>
          <term>Source:</term> Charig, C.R., Webb, D.R., Payne, S.R.
          and Wickham, J.E.A.
          (1986).
          <q>Comparison of treatment of renal calculi by open surgery,
          percutaneous nephrolithotomy, and extracorporeal shockwave
          lithotripsy.</q>
          <em>British Medical Journal</em>, 292(6524), pp.879 <ndash/> 882.
        </p>

        <p>
          <term>Source:</term> Bonovas, S.
          and Piovani, D.
          (2023).
          <q>Simpson's Paradox in Clinical Research: A Cautionary Tale.</q>
          <em>Journal of Clinical Medicine</em>, 12(4), 1633.
        </p>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <p>
          Here are the solutions to the questions posed in the previous section.
          <ol>
            <li>
              <p>
                PCNL would appear to be more successful overall, with an 82.6%
                success rate compared with 78.0% for open surgery.
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                Open surgery has the higher success rate in both groups.
                For small stones, it succeeds in 93.1% of cases compared with
                86.7% for PCNL.
                For large stones, it succeeds in 73.0% of cases compared with
                68.8% for PCNL.
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                The open-surgery group contained a much larger proportion of
                patients with large, harder-to-treat stones.
                The PCNL group contained a much larger proportion of patients
                with small, easier-to-treat stones.
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                Open surgery was being used more often for difficult cases, so
                its overall success rate included many more large-stone cases.
                PCNL was used more often for easier cases, so its overall
                success rate included many more small-stone cases.
              </p>

              <p>
                The overall comparison therefore mixed together differences in
                treatment and differences in the difficulty of the patients
                receiving each treatment.
                The pooled success rates made PCNL look better even though open
                surgery had the higher success rate within both stone-size
                groups.
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                When an important group variable affects both the outcome and
                who receives each treatment, combining everyone together can
                produce a misleading comparison.
                We need to look at the groups separately before interpreting the
                overall result.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
  </exercises>
</chapter>